L001172	\se{1. Introduction}
L001173	
L001174	Nearly a century ago,
L001175	von Neumann~\cite{neumann1929beweis,*von2010proof}
L001176	presented an idea concerning
L001177	the foundations of quantum statistical mechanics:
L001178	Any initial state of an isolated quantum system
L001179	evolves into an equilibrium, so that  quantum dynamics alone,
L001180	without  any
L001181	\emph{ad hoc} assumptions, can explain thermalization
L001182	(see also \cite{GLTZ-2010,Reimann2015-PhysRevLett.115.010403}).
L001183	Although his idea had somewhat been forgotten
L001184	for a long time,
L001185	researches in this direction,
L001186	which show thermalization or equilibration~\footnote{
L001187	A system is said to equilibrate for an observable, if the expectation
L001188	value of the observable approaches
L001189	a stationary value.  A system thermalizes, if it equilibrates for
L001190	almost all (macroscopic) observables and
L001191	their stationary values are given by a Gibbs ensemble.
L001192	We in this Letter will consider equilibration.
L001193	},
L001194	are now flourishing.
L001195	In particular, it has been demonstrated
L001196	that a pure initial state evolves into an equilibrium
L001197	state under quantum dynamics in some general or concrete settings
L001198	\cite{Tasaki1998-PhysRevLett.80.1373,Rigol2008Nature,Reimann2008-PhysRevLett.101.190403,Goldstein-Lebowitz-2010-PhysRevE.81.011109,Reimann_2012isol,ShiraishiTasaki2024JSP,RoosSTT2024}.
L001199	Tasaki~\cite{Tasaki2024freeexpansion}
L001200	recently showed in  a nondegenerate free fermion chain
L001201	that
L001202	\emph{macroscopic} density observables
L001203	equilibrate for \emph{any} initial state.
L001204	
L001205	
L001206	
L001207	
L001208	It should be noted, however, that  the issue of the
L001209	\emph{{\timescale} required for macroscopic
L001210	equilibration has not been well addressed}.
L001211	Statements about equilibration are usually
L001212	proved for ``sufficiently long (but finite) time''
L001213	(see, e.g.,
L001214	Refs.~\cite{Tasaki1998-PhysRevLett.80.1373,Rigol2008Nature,Reimann2008-PhysRevLett.101.190403,Goldstein-Lebowitz-2010-PhysRevE.81.011109,Reimann_2012isol,ShiraishiTasaki2024JSP,RoosSTT2024,Tasaki2024freeexpansion}).
L001215	[The situation is different
L001216	for equilibration of local observables.
L001217	See Section 1.1.]
L001218	Although there are several works
L001219	\cite{GHT2013-PhysRevLett.111.140401,GHT2015,Malabarba-Linden-Short-2014-PhysRevE.90.012121}
L001220	which provide close to optimal {\timescale} of equilibration
L001221	in abstract settings
L001222	\footnote{
L001223	  Von Neumann's original idea turned out to be
L001224	  too optimistic in the sense that it
L001225	  leads to an unrealistically short {\timescale}
L001226	  of equilibration~\cite{GHT2015}.
L001227	},
L001228	there are hardly any good estimates
L001229	for {\timescale}s of equilibration
L001230	in concrete physically realistic short-ranged systems.
L001231	
L001232	In this Letter,
L001233	we address this problem of {\timescale},
L001234	and obtain optimal {\timescale} of macroscopic equilibration for a system of free fermions.
L001235	More precisely, we consider $N$ spinless
L001236	fermions  with uniform nearest-neighbor hopping on a $d$-dimensional lattice $L^d$
L001237	(of linear size $L$, with periodic boundary condition), and its
L001238	macroscopic density observable $\hat\rho$.
L001239	We look into the time evolution of the system from an initial state, and
L001240	focus on the fraction of time duration during which expectation of the density
L001241	$\hat\rho$ macroscopically deviates from its expected average value, $N/L^d$.
L001242	We prove that the fraction goes to zero in the thermodynamic limit
L001243	(i.e.  $L \to \infty$ with $N/L^d$ fixed), for \emph{every} initial state
L001244	and for any $\tau \geq L \times g(L)$, where $g(L)$ is an arbitrary
L001245	function that diverges as $L \to \infty$.
L001246	Since there exist initial states whose equilibration requires time of order $L$,
L001247	our result establishes $O(L)$ as the macroscopic equilibration {\timescale}.
L001248	
L001249	To the best of our knowledge, this is the first time that
L001250	\emph{a realistic {\timescale} of macroscopic equilibration has been rigorously established} for
L001251	isolated physically natural quantum systems.
L001252	
L001253	
L001254	
L001255	\se{1.1. Comparison with works on systems with many conserved quantities}
L001256	
L001257	For systems with many conserved quantities
L001258	(including free fermion systems considered in this Letter),
L001259	equilibration for various {local} observables
L001260	has been studied in
L001261	detail~\cite{Bartell-PhysRevLett.100,Cramer-Eisert-2010,Cazalilla-PhysRevE.85.011133,Sotiriadis_2014,Gluza-et-al-PRL117,Vidmar-Rigol2016JSM,Gluza-Eisert-Farrelly2019,Murthy-Srednicki-PRE100,Gluza-Eisert-Farrelly2019}:
L001262	If the initial state and the dynamics satisfy certain conditions,
L001263	various local observables equilibrate  rather rapidly, and
L001264	this local equilibrium persists up to
L001265	a timescale of the system size $L$.
L001266	On the other hand,
L001267	results on \emph{macroscopic} observables seem to be scarce,
L001268	and it is not clear what happens
L001269	on larger timescales.
L001270	Although physical intuition suggests that the system macroscopically
L001271	equilibrates
L001272	after a timescale of order $L$, and there
L001273	are some works
L001274	which
L001275	partially support this (see, e.g.,
L001276	\cite[eq.~(51)]{Murthy-Srednicki-PRE100}),
L001277	it is not an easy task to establish this fact.
L001278	
L001279	
L001280	This work is  complementary to
L001281	existing works: we focus on a \emph{macroscopic} observable,
L001282	i.e.,  macroscopic density,
L001283	and investigate its behavior
L001284	\emph{beyond the time scale of the system size} $L$.
L001285	We rigorously
L001286	establish that the time average of the macroscopic density approaches
L001287	and remains in
L001288	its equilibrium value after the timescale of order $L$,
L001289	for any initial state.
L001290	
L001291	One more remark is in order.
L001292	Since our system is integrable, it is expected to approach
L001293	equilibrium states described by generalized Gibbs
L001294	ensembles.
L001295	In this sense,
L001296	our system never thermalizes~\cite{Note1}.
L001297	However,
L001298	we hope our analysis would shed some light on understanding
L001299	thermalization in non-integrable systems as well.
L001300	
L001301	
