L001306	\se{2. The setup and the main result}
L001307	
L001308	We now explain our basic setup in more detail and state our main result.
L001309	\se{2.1. Free fermions on a lattice}
L001310	We use the unit $\hbar=1$.
L001311	We consider a system of free fermions on a $d$-dimensional
L001312	hypercubic lattice $\La$ of size $L^d=:V$,
L001313	with periodic boundary conditions.
L001314	We assume $L$ is odd,
L001315	while we can also easily deal with even $L$'s by
L001316	a slight change of  notation.
L001317	The sites of $\La$ are labeled by
L001318	$\xx=(x^1, \dots,x^d) \in {\mathbb Z}^d$, $\abs{x^\m}<L/2$.
L001319	We fix the density $\ba\r>0$,
L001320	and choose the total number $N$ of
L001321	fermions as the largest integer such that $N/V \le \ba\r$.
L001322	The Hamiltonian is given by
L001323	\footnote{For simplicity, we here treat the simplest model, namely,
L001324	  the one with uniform nearest-neighbor hopping.
L001325	Our result should extend to models in
L001326	which $\abs{\Om_\mm(E)}$
L001327	of \eqref{eq-Omega-def}
L001328	is sufficiently regular.
L001329	}
L001330	\begin{align}
L001331		\ha H:= \sum_{\norm{\xx-\yy}=1}\da {\ha c}_\xx \ha c_\yy,
L001332		\qquad \|\xx\| := \Bigl ( \sum_{\mu=1}^{d} (x^\mu)^2 \Bigr )^{1/2}
L001333		,
L001334	  	\label{eq-ham}
L001335	\end{align}
L001336	where $\da c_\xx$ and $c_\xx$
L001337	are spinless fermion's creation and annihilation operators at $\xx\in\La$,
L001338	which satisfy the canonical anticommutation relations
L001339	$
L001340	  \ac {\ha c_\xx}{\ha c_\yy}=  \ac {\da{\ha c}_\xx}{\da{\ha c}_\yy}=0,\,
L001341	  \ac {\ha c_\xx}{\da{\ha c}_\yy}=\d_{\xx,\yy}.
L001342	$
L001343	The creation operator of momentum
L001344	eigenstate $\ket\aa$ of eigenvalue $2\pi\aa/L$,
L001345	where $\aa = (\alpha^1, \dots, \alpha^d)\in {\mathbb Z}^d$ and $\abs{\a^\m}<L/2$, is given by
L001346	\begin{equation}
L001347	  	\da {\ha a}_\aa
L001348	  	:=\f1{\sqrt V}\sum_\xx e^{2\pi i\aa\cdot\xx/L}
L001349	  	\da {\ha c}_\xx
L001350		.
L001351	   	\label{eq-a}
L001352	\end{equation}
L001353	The $N$-particle Hilbert space $\HH$ is spanned
L001354	by $\da {\ha a}_{\aa_1} \cdots \da {\ha a}_{\aa_N}\ketvac$,
L001355	where $\aa_i \neq \aa_j$ for $i \neq j$, and $\aa$'s are
L001356	canonically ordered.
L001357	The Hamiltonian \eqref{eq-ham} can be written as
L001358	\begin{equation}
L001359	  	\ha H=\sum_\aa E_\aa \da{\ha a}_\aa {\ha a}_\aa,
L001360	  	\q
L001361	  	E_\aa:=\sum_{\m=1}^d
L001362	  	2\cos\f{2\pi \a^\m}L.
L001363	  	\label{eq-H-in-a}
L001364	\end{equation}
L001365	
L001366	
L001367	
L001368	Our macroscopic observable is
L001369	the number density
L001370	\begin{equation}
L001371	  	\ha\r_{\ve c}:=
L001372	  	\f1{l^d}
L001373	  	\sum_{\xx\in \bo{\ve c}}\da {\ha c}_\xx \ha c_\xx
L001374	  	\label{eq-rho}
L001375	\end{equation}
L001376	of fermions in a macroscopic box centered at $\ve c\in\La$ and
L001377	of volume $l^d$:
L001378	$
L001379	\bo{\ve c}:=\brac{\xx\in\La; \normi{\xx-\ve c}<\f l2}
L001380	$.
L001381	Here $\normi\xx:=\max\brac{|x^1|, \dots, |x^d|}$.
L001382	To simplify the notation, we have assumed that $l$ is odd.
L001383	We prepare $n^d$ boxes to cover the whole lattice, where
L001384	$n:=\ceil{\tfrac{L}{l}}$
L001385	is the least integer greater than or equal to $\tfrac{L}{l}$.
L001386	The set of centers of the boxes is
L001387	$\CC=\bigl\{jl; -\f{n}2<j\le \f{n}2 , \, j \in \mathbb{Z} \bigr\}^d$,
L001388	where
L001389	the power $d$ denotes the $d$-fold direct set product.
L001390	In what follows, $n$ can be large but still $O(1)$,
L001391	while  $L$ and $l$ are macroscopically large.
L001392	
L001393	\se{2.2. Nonequilibrium subspace}
L001394	We decompose the Hilbert space $\HH$ into
L001395	{\eql} subspace $\HH\eqb$ and {\nql} subspace $\HH\neqb$ as follows.
L001396	Since all $\ha\r_{\ve c}$ mutually commute,
L001397	there is an orthonormal basis $\brac{\ket{e_ j}}$ of $\HH$
L001398	such that each
L001399	$\ket{e_ j}$ is a simultaneous eigenstate of all $\ha\r_{\ve c}$ ($\ve c\in\CC$).
L001400	Let us introduce an operator
L001401	$\wh{\De\r}_{\ve c}:=\ha\r_{\ve c}-N/V$,
L001402	the deviation of density from the mean density, and
L001403	let $\ket{e_ j}$ be an eigenvector:
L001404	$\wh{\De\r}_{\ve c}
L001405	\ket{e_ j}=\l_{j,\ve c}\ket{e_ j}$.
L001406	Define
L001407	\begin{align}
L001408	  	\HH\eqb:=\Span
L001409	  	\brac{\ket{e_j}; \abs{ \l_{j,\ve c} }\le \ep \ba \r
L001410	  	\tx{ for all } j\tx{ and } \ve c},
L001411	\end{align}
L001412	with a small constant $\ep>0$ of our choice
L001413	($\ep=10^{-10}$, for example).
L001414	Define $\HH\neqb:=\HH\eqb^\perp$,
L001415	the orthogonal complement of $\HH\eqb$ in $\HH$.
L001416	When we measure macroscopic observables $\ha\r_{\ve c}$
L001417	($\ve c\in\CC$)
L001418	on a state $\ket\Psi\in\HH\eqb$,
L001419	all measurement outcomes are close ($\le\ep\ba\r$) to $N/V$;
L001420	so we necessarily judge that the system is in equilibrium.
L001421	On the other hand, when we measure $\ha\r_{\ve c}$ on
L001422	$\ket\Psi\in\HH\nqb$,
L001423	the outcome for some $\ha\r_{\ve c}$ may be
L001424	far ($>\ep\ba\r$) from $N/V$; so
L001425	we may find that the system is out of equilibrium.
L001426	Thus, it is natural to introduce the projection operator
L001427	$\ha P\nqb$ onto the subspace $\HH\nqb$ to extract the
L001428	``{\noneq} component'' of any state $\ket\Psi\in\HH$.
L001429	
L001430	
L001431	\se{2.3. Main Result}
L001432	Consider the time evolution
L001433	$\ket{\Psi(t)}$ from an
L001434	\emph{arbitrary} initial state $\ket{\Psi(0)}\in\HH$.
L001435	Let
L001436	\begin{align}
L001437		\bd_a(\tau,L):=
L001438	  	\Bigparen{ \f{\log(\tau/L)}{\tau/L} }^a
L001439	  	+ \Bigparen{ \f{(\log L)^{2d}}L }^a
L001440	  	\label{eq-bd}
L001441	\end{align}
L001442	for $a>0$.
L001443	This is small when $\tau/L$ and $L$ are large,
L001444	and, in particular,
L001445	$\bd_{a}(\tau,L)\to0$ when $\tau/L$ and $L$ go to infinity.
L001446	
L001447	
L001448	Define the set of ``{\noneq} time'' during time interval $[t_1, t_2]$ as
L001449	\begin{align}
L001450	  	\TT^{[t_1, t_2]}_{\delta, \,\ket{\Psi(0)}}
L001451	  	:=
L001452	  	\bigl \{ t\in[t_1, t_2];
L001453	 	\bra{\Psi(t)}\ha P\nqb\ket{\Psi(t)} >  \delta \bigr \}
L001454	\end{align}
L001455	and let $\bigl | \TT^{[t_1, t_2]}_{\delta, \,\ket{\Psi(0)}} \, \bigr |$
L001456	be the total duration (i.e., the Lebesgue measure) of $ \TT^{[t_1, t_2]}_{\delta, \,\ket{\Psi(0)}}$.
L001457	Our main result is the following.
L001458	
L001459	
L001460	\smallskip
L001461	
L001462	
L001463	\bfthm{Theorem~1.}
L001464	The system equilibrates on the {\timescale} of order $L$.
L001465	More precisely, let $\bar\tau(L) = L \times g(L)$, where $g(L)$ is an
L001466	arbitrary function that
L001467	diverges as $L \to \infty$.
L001468	Then, for any $\tau > \bar\tau(L)$ and for \emph{every} $N$-fermion initial state $\ket{\Psi(0)}$,
L001469	the fraction of {\noneq} time during the interval $[0, \tau]$ satisfies,
L001470	for $\delta = \delta_{\!\frac{1}{2}}\!(\tau, L)$,
L001471	\begin{align}
L001472		\frac{1}{\tau} \, \bigl | \TT^{[0, \tau]}_{\delta, \,\ket{\Psi(0)}} \, \bigr | \to 0
L001473	\label{eq-ratio-noneq-time.0}
L001474	\end{align}
L001475	in the thermodynamic limit $L \to \infty$.
L001476	
L001477	
L001478	\medskip
L001479	
L001480	
L001481	Theorem~1 establishes $O(L)$ as the {\timescale} of equilibration
L001482	for this system, because
L001483	as the Lieb-Robinson bound \cite{LiebRobinson72} shows,
L001484	there are initial states whose
L001485	equilibration requires time of $O(L)$ \footnote{
L001486	  For example, when $\bar\rho$ is small, for an initial state where
L001487	  the fermions are concentrated around the origin,
L001488	  there is an $L$-independent  constant $C>0$ such that
L001489	  $\bigl | \sw{\Psi(t)}{\wh{\De\r}_{\ve c}} \bigr | <  \ba\r/2$ for
L001490	  $\abs t< C L$  and  $\ve c = (c,\dots,c)$,
L001491	  where $c$ is the largest integer such that $c\le L/3$.
L001492	}.
L001493	Note that the convergence of \eqref{eq-ratio-noneq-time.0} is uniform in $\ket{\Psi(0)}$;
L001494	the speed of convergence
L001495	does not depend on the choice of $\ket{\Psi(0)}$ for each $L$,
L001496	as Theorem~1$'$ shows.
L001497	
L001498	
L001499	
L001500	
L001501	Theorem~1 is an immediate consequence of the
L001502	following technical theorem,
L001503	because $\bigl | \TT^{[0, \tau]}_{\delta, \,\ket{\Psi(0)}} \, \bigr | \leq  \bigl | \TT^{[-\tau, \tau]}_{\delta, \,\ket{\Psi(0)}} \, \bigr |$.
L001504	
L001505	\smallskip
L001506	
L001507	\bfthm{Theorem~1$'$.}
L001508	Suppose $L > 10^4$ and $ \tau > 2L$.
L001509	For \emph{every} $N$-fermion initial state $\ket{\Psi(0)}$,
L001510	the fraction of {\noneq} time during the interval $[-\tau, \tau]$
L001511	satisfies,
L001512	for $\delta = \delta_{\!\frac{1}{2}}\!(\tau, L)$,
L001513	\begin{align}
L001514		\frac{1}{2 \tau} \, \bigl | \TT^{[-\tau, \tau]}_{\delta, \,\ket{\Psi(0)}} \, \bigr |
L001515	  	\le
L001516	  	K_d \, \f{n^{3d}}{(\ep\ba\r)^2}\;\bd_{\f12}(\ta,L) ,
L001517	  	\label{eq-ratio-noneq-time}
L001518	\end{align}
L001519	where $K_d>0$ is a constant that depends only on $d$.
L001520	
L001521	
L001522	\smallskip
L001523	
L001524	
L001525	
L001526	\se{Remarks}
L001527	
L001528	
L001529	(a) We take advantage of the \emph{macroscopic} nature of our observables
L001530	$\hat \rho_{\ve c}$,
L001531	where $n= \lceil \tfrac{L}{l} \rceil$ is $O(1)$,  in an essential way.
L001532	Remark (a) of Section 4.1 explains how the macroscopic nature is
L001533	utilized in our proof.
L001534	
L001535	
L001536	(b) We are \emph{not} assuming any
L001537	kind of randomness in the system Hamiltonian.
L001538	We are allowing degeneracy in the energy spectrum.
L001539	
L001540	(c)
L001541	Although not explicitly stated, our proof does
L001542	work equally well for other systems,
L001543	with the same result.
L001544	For example, it works for nondegenerate Hamiltonians considered
L001545	in \cite{Tasaki2024freeexpansion}.  The proof works for
L001546	more general (e.g., not necessarily nearest-neighbor)
L001547	hopping of fermions for which an analogue of Lemma~5 holds.
L001548	
L001549	
L001550	(d)
L001551	For fixed $L$, the fraction
L001552	of the nonequilibrium time, i.e., the LHS of
L001553	\eqref{eq-ratio-noneq-time.0},
L001554	and the LHS of \eqref{eq-timeav-bd} below, do not go to
L001555	zero even when $\tau\to \infty$.
L001556	They do vanish in the thermodynamic limit $L\toinf$
L001557	(while keeping $\ta/L>2$).
L001558	
L001559	(e)
L001560	Our system of free fermions never shows equilibration in
L001561	momentum. That is, if we start from an eigenstate of momentum, the system stays in that
L001562	state forever.
L001563	It is of utmost importance to prove results similar to Theorem~1,
L001564	for non-integrable
L001565	systems that show equilibration \emph{both} in space and momentum
L001566	variables.
L001567	
L001568	\medskip
L001569	
L001570	\se{3. Our key estimate for time average}
L001571	Theorem~1$'$ is proved by Proposition~2 below,
L001572	which
L001573	connects the {\noneq} time duration
L001574	$\bigl | \TT^{[-\tau, \tau]}_{\delta, \,\ket{\Psi(0)}} \, \bigr |$
L001575	with a time average of the
L001576	squared deviation $(\wh{\De\r}_{\ve c})^2$.
L001577	We define our time average
L001578	$[\cdots]_\tau$
L001579	for a function of time $t$
L001580	by
L001581	\begin{align}
L001582	  	[\cdots]_\ta:=\intinf \!\!dt \, f_\ta(t) (\cdots) ,
L001583	  	\q f_\ta(t):=\f1{\pi\ta}
L001584		\Bigl ( \f{\sin(t/\ta)}{t/\ta} \Bigr )^2.
L001585	  	\label{eq-timeav-def}
L001586	\end{align}
L001587	The function $f_\ta(t)$ has significant values around the interval
L001588	$[-\ta,\ta]$.
L001589	The reason for our specific choice \eqref{eq-timeav-def} for $f_\tau$ will
L001590	be clear later in Section~4.2.
L001591	For this time average we have the following, which is our key estimate:
L001592	
L001593	
L001594	\smallskip
L001595	
L001596	\bfthm{Proposition~2. }
L001597	Suppose
L001598	$L>10^4$ and $\ta>2L$.
L001599	Then, for \emph{every} $N$-fermion initial state $\ket{\Psi(0)}$
L001600	and for any $c \in {\cal C}$,
L001601	we have
L001602	\begin{align}
L001603	  	\brak{ \bigl \langle \Psi(t) \big | (\wh{\De\r}_{\ve c})^2 \, \big | \Psi(t) \bigr \rangle }_\ta
L001604	  	\le
L001605	  	\f{K_d}3
L001606	  	{n^{2d}}\;
L001607	  	\bd_1(\ta,L),
L001608	  	\label{eq-timeav-bd}
L001609	\end{align}
L001610	where $K_d$ is the same constant as in Theorem~1$'$.
L001611	
L001612	\smallskip
L001613	
L001614	
L001615	
L001616	
L001617	We now show that Proposition~2 implies Theorem~1$'$
L001618	using the following two simple lemmas,
L001619	whose proofs are given in Appendices A and B.
L001620	
L001621	\smallskip
L001622	
L001623	\bfthm{Lemma 3.}
L001624	The expectation values of
L001625	$\hat{P}\nqb$ and $ (\wh{\De\r}_{\ve c}){}^2$ are related by
L001626	\begin{equation}
L001627	  	\sw\Psi {\hat{P}\nqb}
L001628	  	\le
L001629	  	\frac{1}{(\ep\ba\r)^2} \,
L001630	  	\bigl \langle \Psi \big | \,
L001631	  	{  \sum_{\ve c}  (\wh{\De\r}_{\ve c}){}^2}
L001632	  	\, \big | \Psi \bigr \rangle,
L001633	\end{equation}
L001634	for any $\ket\Psi\in\HH$.
L001635	
L001636	\smallskip
L001637	
L001638	\bfthm{Lemma 4.} (``Markov inequality'')
L001639	Suppose a function $X$ of time $t$ satisfies
L001640	$X(t)\ge0$ and $\brak{X(t)}_\ta\le B$ with a
L001641	constant $B>0$.
L001642	Then, for any $t$-independent $X_1>0$,
L001643	the fraction of the total time duration
L001644	with $X(t)>X_1$ in the interval $[-\ta,\ta]$
L001645	is less than $3 B/X_1$.
L001646	
L001647	\smallskip
L001648	
L001649	\se{Proof of Theorem~1$'$, based on Proposition~2}
L001650	This is a standard argument.
L001651	By Proposition~2, Lemma~3, and $\abs \CC\le n^d$,
L001652	we have
L001653	$
L001654	  	\bigl [ \sw{\Psi(t)} {\hat{P}\nqb} \bigr ]_\ta
L001655	  	\le n^d/{(\ep\ba\r)^2}
L001656	  	\times
L001657	  	\paren{\tx{RHS of \eqref{eq-timeav-bd}}}
L001658	  	=:B.
L001659	$
L001660	Then, by Lemma~4 with this $B$
L001661	and $X_1=\bd_{\f12}(\ta,L)=:\d$, we have
L001662	\begin{align}
L001663		\frac{1}{2 \tau} \, \bigl | \TT^{[-\tau, \tau]}_{\delta,
L001664	  \,\ket{\Psi(0)}} \, \bigr |
L001665	  	\le
L001666	  	3
L001667	  	\f{n^d}{(\ep\ba\r)^2}
L001668	  	\f{\paren{\tx{RHS of \eqref{eq-timeav-bd}}}}
L001669	  	{\bd_{\f12}(\ta,L)}.
L001670	\end{align}
L001671	Noting
L001672	$\bd_{1}(\ta,L)\le \nparen{ \bd_{\f12}(\ta,L) }^2$,
L001673	we obtain Theorem~1$'$.
L001674	\qed
L001675	
L001676	\medskip
L001677	
L001678	\se{4. Proof of Proposition~2}
L001679	
L001680	In the rest of the paper, we prove our key estimate, Proposition~2, in several steps.
L001681	
L001682	\se{4.1. Reduction to a single particle problem}
L001683	By the translation symmetry of the system,
L001684	we can focus on one box $\bo{\zz}$ at the origin without loss of generality, and
L001685	we hereafter omit the subscript $\ve c$
L001686	in $\ha \r_{\ve c}$ and $\wh{\De\r}_{\ve c}$.
L001687	We define a Heisenberg operator
L001688	$\ha\r_H(t):=e^{i\hat{H} t}\,\ha{\r}\,e^{-i\hat{H} t}$,
L001689	and accordingly, $\wh{\De\r}_H(t)$.
L001690	Note that the LHS of \eqref{eq-timeav-bd} satisfies
L001691	\begin{align}
L001692		&
L001693		\bigl [ \sw{\Psi(t)}{(\wh{\De\r})^2} \bigr ]_\ta
L001694		=
L001695		\bigl [ \bra{\Psi(0)} (\wh{\De\r}_H(t))^2 \ket{\Psi(0)} \bigr ]_\tau
L001696		\nonumber
L001697		\\
L001698		&
L001699		\quad = \bra{\Psi(0)} \, \bigl [ (\wh{\De\r}_H(t))^2 \bigr ]_\tau \ket{\Psi(0)}
L001700		.
L001701		\label{eq-LHSprop2-rewrite1}
L001702	\end{align}
L001703	
L001704	
L001705	We work in the momentum representation.
L001706	The observable $\ha\r$ of
L001707	\eqref{eq-rho} is rewritten in terms of
L001708	$\da {\ha a}_\aa$ and ${\ha a}_\aa$ as
L001709	\begin{align}
L001710	  	\ha\r
L001711	  	&=
L001712	  	\f1{V}\sum_{\aa,\bb}
L001713	  	\ti w(\aa-\bb)\da {\ha a}_{\aa}{\ha a}_{\bb},
L001714	  	\label{eq-rho-in-a}
L001715	    \\
L001716	  	\ti w(\mm)
L001717	  	&=
L001718	  	\f1{l^d}\sum_{\xx; \norminf \xx<\f l2}
L001719	  	e^{-2 \pi i\mm\cdot\xx / L}
L001720	  	=
L001721	    \prod_{\m=1}^d \ti w_1(m^\m),
L001722	    \label{eq-tiw}
L001723	\end{align}
L001724	where
L001725	$\ti w_1(k):={\sin(\f{\pi lk}L)}\big/({l\sin\f{\pi k}L})$ for $k \neq 0$,
L001726	and $\ti w_1(0) := 1$.
L001727	Note that $\ti w(\zz)=1$.
L001728	
L001729	
L001730	
L001731	
L001732	Since the Hamiltonian \eqref{eq-H-in-a} is diagonal
L001733	in momentum representaiton,
L001734	we have
L001735	$
L001736	  e^{it\ha H}\da{\ha a}_\aa =
L001737	  e^{itE_\aa} \da{\ha a}_\aa e^{it\ha H}$ and
L001738	${\ha a}_\bb e^{-it\ha H}=e^{-itE_\bb}e^{-it\ha H}{\ha a}_\bb
L001739	$.
L001740	Therefore, from
L001741	\eqref{eq-rho-in-a}
L001742	we have
L001743	\begin{align}
L001744		\ha\r_H(t)
L001745	  	=
L001746	  	\f1V\sum_{\mm} \ti w(\mm)\ha F_t(\mm),
L001747	    \label{eq-rho-in-a-2}
L001748	\end{align}
L001749	where
L001750	\begin{equation}
L001751		\ha F_t(\mm):=  \sum_{\bb}
L001752		e^{-it\ti E_{\bb,\mm}}
L001753		\hat{a}^\dagger_{\bb+\ve m}\hat{a}_{\bb},
L001754		\quad
L001755		\ti E_{\bb,\mm}:=E_\bb-E_{\bb+\mm} .
L001756		\label{eq-Etilde-def.11}
L001757	\end{equation}
L001758	We observe that the
L001759	time evolution operator
L001760	$e^{-it\ha H}$ in $\ha\r_H(t)$ has disappeared,
L001761	or ``pair-annihilated.''
L001762	As a result, the analysis of
L001763	$\ha\r_H(t)$ has essentially been reduced to that of a
L001764	single particle with energy spectrum $\ti E$.
L001765	We also have
L001766	\begin{align}
L001767		\wh{\De\r}_H(t)=\ha\r_H(t)-\frac{N}{V}
L001768		= \frac{1}{V}\sum_{\mm\ne\zz}\ti w(\mm)\ha F_t(\mm),
L001769		\label{eq-drt-by-mneq0}
L001770	\end{align}
L001771	because $\ti w(\zz)=1$ and
L001772	$\ha F_t(\zz)=\sum_{\bb}\da {\ha a}_\bb {\ha a}_\bb=N$ on $\HH$.
L001773	
L001774	
L001775	
L001776	\se{Remarks}
L001777	(a)
L001778	$\ti w_1$ satisfies the bound
L001779	\footnote{
L001780	  Eq.~\eqref{eq-w1-bound.11} holds for $m=0$
L001781	  because $\ti w_1(0) = 1$ by definition.
L001782	  For $m \neq 0$, we note $\ti w_1(m)$ is even in $m$, and we assume $m >0$.
L001783	  We note that $\ti w_1(m) = (\sin\f{\pi l m}L)/(l\sin\f{\pi m}L)
L001784	  = (1/l) \sum_{x=-(l-1)/2}^{(l-1)/2} e^{- 2 \pi i m x/L}$.
L001785	  Taking the absolute value of both sides proves $|\ti w_1(m)| \leq 1$.
L001786	  Also, using $|\sin\f{\pi l m}L| \leq 1$ and $|\sin\f{\pi m}L | \geq \frac{2m}{L}$
L001787	  proves $|\ti w_1(m)| \leq L/(2 m l) \leq n/(2m)$.
L001788	}
L001789	\begin{equation}
L001790		\abs{\ti w_1(m)}
L001791		\le 1\we \f n{2\abs{m}}.
L001792		\label{eq-w1-bound.11}
L001793	\end{equation}
L001794	where $a\we b:=\min\brac{a,b}$.
L001795	This
L001796	guarantees that only
L001797	$m$'s with small $\norminf{\mm}$ are relevant in the sum over $\mm$
L001798	in \eqref{eq-drt-by-mneq0},
L001799	because $n$ is a constant independent of $L$
L001800	due to macroscopic nature of our observable $\ha\r$.
L001801	
L001802	
L001803	
L001804	(b)
L001805	We have
L001806	$\tilde{E}_{\beta, \mm} = O(\norminf{\mm|}/L)$, as is
L001807	seen from \eqref{eq-tiE} below.
L001808	Since $\norminf{\mm}$ is effectively small
L001809	as was just remarked,
L001810	we expect
L001811	that the time average of $(\De\r)$ over a {\timescale}
L001812	$\tau \gtrsim O(L)$ is small and that
L001813	the equilibration {\timescale} is
L001814	$O(L)$.
L001815	
L001816	
L001817	
L001818	(c) However, to obtain precise information on the fraction of
L001819	nonequilibrium time
L001820	as shown in Theorem~1$'$,
L001821	we must
L001822	analyze not only $(\De\r)$ but $(\De\r)^2$.
L001823	For this, we must deal with time
L001824	averages of $e^{-it(\ti E_{\bb,\mm}-\ti E_{\cc,\ve n})}$,
L001825	which depends on \emph{two} energies,
L001826	in \eqref{eq-FFexp.2} below.
L001827	The following proof, based on the identity \eqref{eq-decomp-meas},
L001828	is so devised as to control time averages of this type efficiently.
L001829	As a result,  we do not need minute
L001830	control of possible degeneracies
L001831	of $\ti E_{\bb,\ve m}$'s \footnote{Note that nondegeneracy of  $\ti E_{\bb,\ve m}$
L001832	is equivalent to the so called ``non-resonance'' condition for $E_{\bb}$'s in our
L001833	setting.}; some good bounds on the ``number of states''
L001834	$|\Omega_{\mm}(E)|$ [see \eqref{eq-Omega-def}]
L001835	are sufficient.
L001836	
L001837	
L001838	
L001839	
L001840	\se{4.2. Key formula for $\bigbrak{ (\wh{\De\r}_H(t))^2 }_\ta$}
L001841	
L001842	Let us express  $\bigbrak{ (\wh{\De\r}_H(t))^2 }_\ta$ in a more tractable
L001843	form.
L001844	Since $\wh{\De\r}_H$ is Hermitian,
L001845	we have from \eqref{eq-drt-by-mneq0},
L001846	\begin{align}
L001847	  	&{\bigbrak{ (\wh{\De\r}_H(t))^2 }_\ta}
L001848	  	=
L001849	    \frac{1}{V^2}
L001850	    \sum_{\mm,\ve n\ne\zz} \ti w(\mm)\ti w(\ve n)
L001851	    \bigbrak{ \ha F_t(\mm)\ha F_t(\ve n)^\dagger }_\ta,
L001852	    \label{eq-to-FF}
L001853	  	\\
L001854	  	&\bigbrak{ \ha F_t(\mm)\ha F_t(\ve n)^\dagger }_\ta
L001855	  	=
L001856	  	\sum_{\bb,\cc}
L001857	  	\bigbrak{
L001858	  	e^{-it(\ti E_{\bb,\mm}-\ti E_{\cc,\ve n})}
L001859	  	}_\ta
L001860	    \da {\ha a}_{\bb+\mm} {\ha a}_{\bb} \da {\ha a}_{\cc} {\ha a}_{\cc+\ve n}.
L001861	    \label{eq-FFexp.2}
L001862	\end{align}
L001863	We now introduce our \emph{key identity}: for any $E'$ and $E''$,
L001864	\begin{align}
L001865		&
L001866		\bigbrak{
L001867	  	e^{-it( E' - E'')}
L001868	  	}_\ta
L001869	  	= \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f_\tau(t) \,
L001870		e^{-it( E' - E'')}
L001871		\nonumber \\
L001872		&
L001873		=
L001874		\f\ta2
L001875	    \intinf \dd E\;
L001876	    I\bigbrak{\nabs{E'-E }\le\f1\ta}\,
L001877	    I\bigbrak{\nabs{E-E'' } \le\f1\ta},
L001878	    \label{eq-decomp-meas}
L001879	\end{align}
L001880	where $I[\;\cdot\;]$ is the indicator function that takes value
L001881	1 if the argument is true and 0 otherwise.
L001882	
L001883	
L001884	\se{Remark}
L001885	This identity is a special case of a
L001886	well-known property of
L001887	Fourier transform: if $f(t) = g(t)^2$, their Fourier transforms
L001888	satisfy $\widehat{f} = \tx{{const.}} \, \hat{g} *  \hat{g}$, where $*$
L001889	denotes the convolution.
L001890	This general property enables us to
L001891	take the sum over two energies $E'$ and $E''$ independently.
L001892	Our specific choice \eqref{eq-timeav-def} of $f_\tau$ leads to
L001893	$\hat{g}(E) \propto  I[ |E| \le 1/\tau]$,
L001894	which is most convenient for the present analysis.
L001895	
L001896	Applying identity \eqref{eq-decomp-meas} to \eqref{eq-FFexp.2}, we have
L001897	\begin{align}
L001898	  	&\bigbrak{ \ha F_t(\mm)\ha F_t(\ve n)^\dagger }_\ta
L001899	    =
L001900	    \f\ta2
L001901	    \sum_{\bb,\cc}
L001902	    \intinf \dd E\;
L001903	  	I\bigbrak{\nabs{E-\ti E_{\bb,\mm}}\le\f1\ta}
L001904	    \nn
L001905	    &\qq \qq\times
L001906	 	I\bigbrak{\nabs{E-\ti E_{\cc,\ve n}}\le\f1\ta}\;
L001907	  	\da {\ha a}_{\bb+\mm} {\ha a}_{\bb} \da {\ha a}_{\cc} {\ha a}_{\cc+\ve n}
L001908	    \nonumber \\
L001909	    & =
L001910	    \f\ta2
L001911	    \intinf \!\! \dd E\,
L001912	    \bigparen{ \sum_{\bb\in\Om_\mm(E)} \!\!
L001913	    \da {\ha a}_{\bb+\mm} {\ha a}_{\bb} }
L001914	    \bigparen{ \sum_{\cc\in\Om_{\ve n}(E)} \!\!
L001915	    \da {\ha a}_{\cc} {\ha a}_{\cc+\ve n} },
L001916	    \label{eq-FF}
L001917	\end{align}
L001918	where
L001919	\begin{equation}
L001920		\Om_{\mm}(E):=\bigbrac{\bb; \;
L001921	  	\abs{\b^\m}<\f L2,
L001922	  	\nabs{ E-\ti E_{\bb,\mm} }<\f1\ta}
L001923	  	\label{eq-Omega-def}
L001924	\end{equation}
L001925	consists of the indices $\bb$ of ``energy levels
L001926	$\ti E_{\bb,\mm}$
L001927	near the energy $E$,'' with fixed $\mm$.
L001928	Substituting \eqref{eq-FF} to \eqref{eq-to-FF}, we finally have
L001929	\begin{align}
L001930	  	&
L001931	  	\bigbrak{ (\wh{\De\r}_H(t))^2 }_\ta
L001932	  	=
L001933	  	\f\ta2
L001934	    \intinf\dd E\;
L001935	    \ha G(E)\da{\ha G}(E),
L001936	    \label{eq-dr2t-final}
L001937	    \\
L001938	    &\hat{G}(E):=
L001939	    \f1V
L001940	    \sum_{\mm\ne\zz} \ti w(\mm)
L001941	    \sum_{\bb\in\Om_\mm(E)}
L001942	    \da {\ha a}_{\bb+\mm} {\ha a}_{\bb} .
L001943	    \label{eq-dr2t-final-2}
L001944	\end{align}
L001945	
L001946	
L001947	\se{4.3. Our key estimate}
L001948	
L001949	We use the following simple but useful inequality for
L001950	the RHS of \eqref{eq-LHSprop2-rewrite1}:
L001951	\begin{align}
L001952	  	\sw{\Psi(0)}
L001953	  	{\bigbrak{ (\wh{\De\r}_H(t))^2 }_\ta}
L001954	  	& \le
L001955	  	\bignormop{\bigbrak{ (\wh{\De\r}_H(t))^2 }_\ta}.
L001956	  	\label{eq-drt-le-drhto}
L001957	\end{align}
L001958	It thus suffices to bound the operator norm of \eqref{eq-dr2t-final}.
L001959	We have
L001960	$\| \hat{G}(E) \|_{\rm op} \le \f1V \sum_{\mm\ne\zz} \abs{\ti w(\mm)} \abs{\Om_\mm(E)}
L001961	$
L001962	from \eqref{eq-dr2t-final-2} and
L001963	$\nnormop{\da {\ha a}_{\bb+\mm} {\ha a}_{\bb}} =1$.
L001964	Using the Schwarz inequality
L001965	$\f\ta{2}\intinf\dd E\;
L001966	\abs{\Om_\mm(E)}
L001967	\abs{\Om_{\ve n}(E)}
L001968	\le \sqrt{\II_\mm \II_{\ve n}}$,
L001969	where
L001970	\begin{equation}
L001971		\II_\mm:=
L001972		\f\ta{2}
L001973		\intinf\dd E\;
L001974		\abs{\Om_\mm(E)}^2
L001975		,
L001976		\label{eq-IIdef.11}
L001977	\end{equation}
L001978	we obtain from \eqref{eq-dr2t-final},
L001979	\begin{align}
L001980	  	\bignormop{\bigbrak{ (\wh{\De\r}_H(t))^2 }_\ta}
L001981	  	&\le
L001982	  	\bigl ( \f1{V}
L001983	  	\sum_{\mm\ne\zz}
L001984	  	\abs{ \ti w(\mm) }
L001985	    \sqrt{\II_\mm} \, \bigr )^2
L001986	    .
L001987	 	\label{eq-drhto-ineq}
L001988	\end{align}
L001989	Our task has thus been reduced to estimating
L001990	$\abs{\Om_\mm(E)}$, and its square integral $\II_\mm$
L001991	appearing in \eqref{eq-drhto-ineq}.
L001992	Note that $\abs{\Om_\mm(E)}$ is a ``density of states (DOS),''
L001993	or rather ``number of states,''
L001994	of single particle energy eigenvalues $\tilde{E}$.
L001995	
L001996	
L001997	\se{4.4. Estimating $\abs{\Om_\mm(E)}$ for $d=1$}
L001998	Let us give a concrete estimate of
L001999	$\abs{\Om_\mm(E)}$ for $d=1$.
L002000	We write $\aa=\a$, $\mm=m$, etc.
L002001	Let $m\ne0$.
L002002	Because \eqref{eq-H-in-a}
L002003	and \eqref{eq-Etilde-def.11} implies
L002004	\begin{align}
L002005	\ti E_{\b,m}
L002006	=C_m \sin\bigbrak{\f{2\pi}{L} \bigparen{\b+\f m2}},
L002007	\q C_m=4\sin\f{\pi m}{L},
L002008	\label{eq-tiE}
L002009	\end{align}
L002010	we can write
L002011	$\abs{\Om_m(E)}=\bigabs{\om_m(\f E{C_m},\f1{C_m\ta})}$,
L002012	where
L002013	\begin{align}
L002014	  \om_m(x,\d):=\bigbrac{\b;\; \abs\b<\f L2,
L002015	  \Bigabs{x-\sin\bigbrak{\f{2\pi}{L}\bigparen{\b+\f m2}} }
L002016	  \le \d }.
L002017	  \label{eq-om}
L002018	\end{align}
L002019	It is obvious that $\abs{\om_m(x,\d)}=0$
L002020	for $\abs x> 1+\d$.
L002021	For  $|x| \leq 1 + \d$, we have the following Lemma,
L002022	whose proof is given in Appendix~C.
L002023	
L002024	\smallskip
L002025	
L002026	\bfthm{Lemma 5} (Upper bound on the $\abs{\Om_\mm(E)}$ for $d=1$)
L002027	If $m\ne0$, $0<\d \le1/2$ and $\abs x\le 1+\d$, we have
L002028	\begin{align}
L002029	  	\abs{\om_m(x,\d)}
L002030	  	\le
L002031	  	\f{L\d}{\sqrt{1-\{\abs x\we (1-\d)\}^2}}+2 .
L002032	  	\label{eq-DOS}
L002033	\end{align}
L002034	$\abs{ \Om_m(E) }$ obeys this upper bound with
L002035	$x=E/C_m$ and $\d =1/(C_m\ta)$.
L002036	
L002037	\smallskip
L002038	
L002039	
L002040	\se{4.5. Estimating $\II_\mm$}
L002041	
L002042	We now estimate $\II_\mm$.
L002043	For $d=1$, a direct computation using Lemma~5 yields the following:
L002044	
L002045	\smallskip
L002046	
L002047	\bfthm{Lemma 6} (Upper bound on $\II_m$ for $d=1$)
L002048	Suppose $d=1$,  $m\ne0$, $L>10^4$ and $\ta>2L$. Then we have
L002049	\begin{align}
L002050	  	\II_m\le
L002051	  	L^2 \, \f{\log (\abs{C_m}\ta)}
L002052	  	{\abs{C_m}\ta} +4L.
L002053	\end{align}
L002054	
L002055	\smallskip
L002056	
L002057	For $d \geq 2$, we observe that
L002058	$\II_\mm$ are bounded by $\II_m$ of $d=1$:
L002059	
L002060	
L002061	\medskip
L002062	
L002063	\bfthm{Lemma~7} (Upper bound on $\II_\mm$ for $d \geq 2$)
L002064	Suppose $d \geq 2$, $\mm\ne\zz$, $L>10^4$ and $\ta>2L$. Then, we have
L002065	\begin{align}
L002066		\II_\mm
L002067		\leq \f{V^2}{L^2} \II_{\norm \mm_\infty},
L002068		\quad \norminf\mm = \max_{1\le\m\le d} \abs{m^\m},
L002069		\label{eq-Imd>2-by-d=1}
L002070	\end{align}
L002071	where $\II_{\norm \mm_\infty}$ on the RHS denotes $\II$ of $d=1$.
L002072	
L002073	\smallskip
L002074	
L002075	Proofs of these Lemmas are given in Appendices~D and E.
L002076	
L002077	
L002078	\se{4.6. Estimating
L002079	  $\bigl \| \bigbrak{ (\wh{\De\r}_H(t))^2 }_\ta \bigr \|_{\rm op}$}
L002080	
L002081	Using bounds on $\II_\mm$ given by Lemmas~6 and 7,
L002082	we now bound the RHS of \eqref{eq-drhto-ineq}.
L002083	
L002084	\smallskip
L002085	
L002086	\bfthm{Lemma~8}
L002087	For $L>10^4$ and $\ta>2L$,
L002088	we have
L002089	\begin{align}
L002090	  	\f1V\sum_{\mm\ne\zz}\abs{\ti w(\mm)}\sqrt{\II_\mm}
L002091	  	\le
L002092	    K_{d}' \, n^d \,
L002093	  	\d_{\f12}(\ta,L),
L002094	\end{align}
L002095	where $K_d'>0$ depends only on $d$.
L002096	
L002097	\smallskip
L002098	
L002099	The proof of Lemma~8 is given in Appendix~F.
L002100	
L002101	
L002102	\smallskip
L002103	
L002104	We complete the proof of Proposition~2 by
L002105	Eqs.~\eqref{eq-LHSprop2-rewrite1},
L002106	\eqref{eq-drt-le-drhto}, and
L002107	\eqref{eq-drhto-ineq} and  Lemma~8,
L002108	together with
L002109	$\nparen{\d_{\f12}(\ta,L)}^2\le 2\d_{1}(\ta,L)$.
L002110	
L002111	
L002112	
L002113	\medskip
L002114	
L002115	\se{5. Conclusion}
L002116	
L002117	In this Letter, we considered
L002118	a  class of translation-invariant free-fermion systems
L002119	(including those with uniform nearest neighbor hopping)
L002120	on a $d$-dimensional $L \times \cdots \times L$ hypercubic lattice.
L002121	We proved that, starting from an \emph{arbitrary} pure initial state,
L002122	the system equilibrates with respect to
L002123	the coarse-grained density within a {\timescale} of order $L$.
L002124	This scaling is optimal,
L002125	since there exist initial states whose equilibration requires time of order $L$.
L002126	Our result establishes $O(L)$ as the \emph{equilibration {\timescale}} for the macroscopic density.
L002127	This will be the first time  that
L002128	a {\timescale} of macroscopic equilibration has been rigorously established for
L002129	isolated physically natural quantum systems.
L002130	Although our current result is restricted to free fermion systems (which are integrable),
L002131	we hope our analysis will shed some light on
L002132	our understanding of macroscopic equilibration in non-integrable quantum systems.
